ef 17592186433942 13194139534719 17592186045417 true] [17592186142945 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433943 13194139534719 17592186045417 true] [17592186143062 1050 420.0 13194139534719 17592186045417 true] [17592186143499 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534719 17592186045417 true] [17592186143499 1071 "![](images/7f/7f83fbd712fa6864377a93e91c0fc9e50c7bfd3c3a9e176e9dfe30cbb5b978ea.png)\n\nUse the model above to calculate $\\frac{2}{3} \\div 3$." 13194139534719 17592186045417 false] [17592186143499 1071 "![](images/7f/7f83fbd712fa6864377a93e91c0fc9e50c7bfd3c3a9e176e9dfe30cbb5b978ea.png)\n\nUse the model above to calculate $\\frac{2}{3}÷3.$" 13194139534719 17592186045417 true] [17592186143499 1074 "Since we need to divide by $3$, we split the whole into $3$ equal parts horizontally.\n\n![](images/9b/9bf718ede82077383cd81314c025a1752820e5d39c20cf3b45700e721bd16f3f.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we obtain that the shaded part represents $\\frac{2}{9}$ of the whole.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}÷3=\\frac{2}{9}.\n$$" 13194139534719 17592186045417 true] [17592186143542 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534719 17592186045417 true] [17592186143542 1074 "We have $\\frac{2}{3}$ on the left and $\\frac{2}{12}$ on the right.\n\n![](images/6f/6f6687c4190a54e9974dc600ced3a2662718cce1bcd5d49410a994259ac06e58.png)\n\nThe division operation splits each vertical line into $4$ equal parts. So the shaded parts on the left are $4$ times larger than the shaded parts on the right. \n\nTherefore, the division problem shown is\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}÷4=\\frac{2}{12}.\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $4.$" 13194139534719 17592186045417 true] [17592186144002 1064 "When we divide a fraction by a whole number, say ${\\color{red}{2}}$, the idea is to\n\n- split the fraction into ${\\color{red}{2}}$ equal parts, and\n- determine the fraction that makes up each part.\n\nFor example, suppose we wish to find the value of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{5} \\div 2\n$$\n\nLet's start by drawing the fraction model for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac45\n$$\n\n![](images/d1/d159ff7e61ae308f27dd561cc8b219f0fc5b5fa46135ebff70ed15558186280b.png)\n\nWe need to divide this fraction by ${\\color{red}{2}}$. To do this, we draw a horizontal line through our fraction that splits it into $\\color{red}2$ *equal parts*.\n\n![](images/87/87c0a9dad9ab63de7858e8857e412b4d6ce849dfc0b02a172a5debe420c5baf6.png)\n\nWe only need to determine the fraction that makes up one of the parts. Therefore, we can focus on only one row (i.e., the bottom row).\n\nNow, we remove all the shaded parts not in the bottom row.\n\n![](images/54/54a3760d69e7622afa800e4fe107cdafc7069a1fd31138a9ad6c63e8cf6f8f1f.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we obtain that the shaded part represents\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{10}\n$$\n\nof the whole. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{5} \\div 2 = \\dfrac{4}{10} \\,\n$$" 13194139534719 17592186045417 false] [17592186144002 1064 "When we divide a fraction by a whole number, say ${\\color{red}{2}},$ the idea is to \n\n- split the fraction into ${\\color{red}{2}}$ equal parts, and\n\n- determine the fraction that makes up each part.\n\nFor example, suppose we wish to find the value of \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{5} \\div 2.\n$$\n\nLet's start by drawing the fraction model for $\\dfrac45\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/d1/d159ff7e61ae308f27dd561cc8b219f0fc5b5fa46135ebff70ed15558186280b.png)\n\nWe need to divide this fraction by ${\\color{red}{2}}.$ To do this, we draw a horizontal line through our fraction that splits it into $\\color{red}2$ *equal parts*.\n\n![](images/87/87c0a9dad9ab63de7858e8857e412b4d6ce849dfc0b02a172a5debe420c5baf6.png)\n\nWe only need to determine the fraction that makes up one of the parts. Therefore, we can focus on only one row (i.e., the bottom row).\n\nNow, we remove all the shaded parts not in the bottom row. \n\n![](images/54/54a3760d69e7622afa800e4fe107cdafc7069a1fd31138a9ad6c63e8cf6f8f1f.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we obtain that the shaded part represents $\\dfr